# @PROJECT_DISPLAY_NAME@

Installable C++23 library: `@PROJECT_NAME@::@PROJECT_NAME@`.

## Development

Install CMake 3.24+, Ninja, Git, and a compiler supporting C++23. On macOS,
install the Xcode command line tools. On Windows, install Visual Studio C++
build tools and run commands from a Developer Command Prompt.

```sh
./setup.sh
./run.sh
```

On Windows use `setup.bat` and `run.bat`. Pass `release` to either launcher
for a release build; debug is the default. Manual builds:

```sh
cmake --preset debug
cmake --build --preset debug
cmake --preset release
cmake --build --preset release
```

`scaffold` is destructive, one-time project initialization. `setup` is repeatable
machine setup. `run` configures, builds, and runs the CMake `run` target.
Another developer cloning this generated project only needs setup and run.
Setup never removes build output. To clean, remove `build/` yourself.

## Dependencies

Edit `vcpkg.json` to add dependencies, then rerun setup. The starter dependency
is `fmt`. Setup clones the latest vcpkg into ignored `external/vcpkg/`, removes
its `.git`, bootstraps it, and installs the manifest into `vcpkg_installed/`.
Later setup runs reuse that copy. There is no pinned revision or submodule.
Remove `external/vcpkg/` manually if you need a fresh copy.

Dependency installation belongs to setup; run does not install dependencies.
Presets disable vcpkg's automatic manifest installation for this reason.
vcpkg chooses its native triplet. To override it, set `VCPKG_DEFAULT_TRIPLET`
in your environment before setup and keep it set during configuration and run.
Custom presets must use the same triplet and `VCPKG_INSTALLED_DIR` as setup.

GitHub Actions builds this project on macOS and Windows using the same setup
and development presets. Its workflow is independent of `.template/`.
The `Required validation` check must pass for `main` and pull requests targeting
`main`; failures on other branches produce warnings. If this repository retains
`.github/rulesets/protect-main.json`, import it in GitHub Settings → Rules →
Rulesets to enforce this check before merging. Tracking the file alone does not
activate branch protection.

## Finishing generation

Once you are satisfied with the generated project and no longer need
re-scaffolding, you may remove `.template/`, `scaffold.sh`, and `scaffold.bat`.
No build or development script depends on them. Until then, re-scaffolding
requires destructive confirmation and erases generated source changes.

## Library development

`run` runs the example program. Public headers live in
`include/@PROJECT_NAMESPACE@/`; implementation and private headers live in
`src/`. The DLL export header is generated in the build tree and installed
with the public headers. Mark public symbols with `@PROJECT_MACRO@_EXPORT`.

Examples and CTest tests default to enabled only when this is the top-level
project. Consumers using `add_subdirectory()` get the library alone; override
`@PROJECT_MACRO@_BUILD_EXAMPLES` and `@PROJECT_MACRO@_BUILD_TESTS` as needed.

```sh
cmake --build --preset debug
ctest --preset debug
# Static is the default. Switch to shared (a DLL on Windows):
cmake --preset debug -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON
cmake --build --preset debug
ctest --preset debug
cmake --install build/debug --prefix /path/to/install --config Debug
```

Switch back with `-DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF`. Install a release build by replacing
`debug`/`Debug` with `release`/`Release`. Installation includes the library,
headers, export header, targets, package config, and version config.

## Consuming the installed package

```cmake
find_package(@PROJECT_NAME@ 0.1 CONFIG REQUIRED)
add_executable(consumer main.cpp)
target_link_libraries(consumer PRIVATE @PROJECT_NAME@::@PROJECT_NAME@)
```

```cpp
#include <@PROJECT_NAMESPACE@/@PROJECT_NAMESPACE@.hpp>
int main() { return @PROJECT_NAMESPACE@::add(2, 3) == 5 ? 0 : 1; }
```

Set `CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH` to the install prefix and make `fmt` available, such as
through the consumer's own vcpkg manifest and toolchain. The package resolves
`fmt` using `find_dependency`. No original source checkout is needed.
For shared builds on Windows, put the installed `bin/` and dependency DLL
locations on `PATH` when running the consumer. Other platforms likewise need
runtime libraries on their loader search path if installed outside standard
locations.
